{-
   What the three legacy-SSE markers refuse, and where each refusal is made.

   Five of them are read off the declaration, which is the only place that can name the attribute
   the author wrote. The sixth is not here and cannot be: a wide vector inside a marked function, and
   a SHA instruction inside an *unmarked* one, are both questions about a body after inlining, so
   they are reported by the x64 backend - see `checkLegacyVectorEncoding` in machine_vector.cpp.

   The second of those pair is the one worth knowing about. `@x86_legacy_sse` marks a
   `LowerFunction`, and an inlined body has none - so a marked helper spliced into an unmarked caller
   would carry its SHA instructions into a VEX-encoded body with nothing left to say so. That check
   is the root test the deleted `markLegacyVectorEncodings` used to find its own starting set, kept
   as an assertion now that nothing infers the answer.
-}

import Native

-- Two of the six conventions the lower IR has are the compiler's own choice between internal shapes,
-- and one describes what a syscall leaves alone rather than how anything is entered. Naming one is
-- writing an implementation detail down as though it were an interface.
@convention(simple)
fn internalShape(x: Int) -> Int = x

@convention(system)
fn kernelSide(x: Int) -> Int = x

-- A name no convention has.
@convention(fastcall)
fn unknownName(x: Int) -> Int = x

-- The attribute takes exactly one argument, and it is a name rather than an expression.
@convention
fn noArgument(x: Int) -> Int = x

@convention(clobber, sysv)
fn twoArguments(x: Int) -> Int = x

-- Two of them on one declaration say two different things about how one function is entered.
@convention(clobber)
@convention(sysv)
fn twice(x: Int) -> Int = x

{-
   And the encoding attribute, which takes no arguments at all.

   `@x86_legacy_sse(entry)` is the spelling an earlier design had, where one attribute meant both
   "encode this body without a prefix" and "clear the vector state on the way in". They are facts
   about different sets of functions - every function of a region is encoded legacy, and only its
   entry points may clear the state, since a helper's vector argument would be destroyed - so the
   second is `X86.vzeroupper()`, a call the author places. Reserving the argument form here is what
   keeps the attribute meaning one thing.
-}
@x86_legacy_sse(entry)
fn withArgument(x: Int) -> Int = x

fn main() -> Int = 0
